SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers Enrique Dussel's primary texts and close exegetical
literature articulating his critique of Eurocentrism. Central is the
"myth of modernity" thesis (most fully developed in The Invention of
the Americas, 1992/1995): the claim that Europe's self-understanding
as the autonomous origin of modern reason conceals a violent founding
event — 1492 — by which Europe constituted itself as "center" of an
emerging world-system through the conquest, occlusion, and sacrificial
"justification" of the non-European Other. Dussel argues that
pre-1492 Europe was peripheral within a polycentric inter-regional
system dominated by Islamic, Indian, and Chinese cultural-economic
circuits, and that only the colonization of the Americas allowed
Europe to become "center." The normative payoff is transmodernity:
not anti-modern rejection but a pluriversal project that takes up
modernity's emancipatory rationality while affirming the alterity of
the colonial Other from "the underside of history."

Key primary works in this domain include The Invention of the
Americas (1995), the Frankfurt lectures "Europa, modernidad y
eurocentrismo" (1993), the programmatic "World-System and 'Trans'-
Modernity" (2002), "A New Age in the History of Philosophy" (2009),
and the architectonic statement of his ethics (1997, 2013). Key
exegetical and critical commentaries (Mendieta & Alcoff 2000; Mills
2018; Allen & Mendieta 2021; Lange 1998; Pachón Soto 2012; Vargas
2005; Deere 2017) clarify Dussel's historiographical claims, contest
his treatment of indigenous and feminist alterity, and assess the
coherence of transmodernity.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the project's primary-text foundation: any
literature review on Dussel's critique of Eurocentrism must establish
what Dussel actually argues in The Invention of the Americas and the
1492 lectures, what he means by "myth," and how transmodernity is
supposed to follow from the world-system reframing.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
English-language close exegesis of Dussel's specific historiographical
claims about pre-1492 Europe (e.g., his reliance on Abu-Lughod and
Frank) remains thin; most secondary literature focuses on the ethical
or political payoff rather than the world-history premises.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Lead with Dussel's primary 1492/myth-of-modernity texts; use the
exegetical commentaries to triangulate; reserve Lange and Vargas for
the section on internal critique.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Dussel's primary articulation: 5 papers — myth of modernity, 1492,
  transmodernity as positive program
- Sympathetic exegesis: 4 papers — Mendieta/Alcoff, Mills, Allen/
  Mendieta, Pachón Soto reconstructing Dussel's framework
- Internal/feminist critique: 2 papers — Lange and Vargas pressing
  Dussel on indigenous alterity and Eurocentrism's residue
- Comparative/contextual: 2 papers — Irvine and Sánchez situating
  Dussel against Apel/Hinkelammert and Western ontology
====================================================================
}

